\section{Enterprise Hardware Engineering Technology: Complete Implementation Package}

\subsection*{Lead Systems Architect \& Independent Designer: C.~Matthew Blackwell}

\section{System Overview}

This document defines the complete end-to-end implementation package for modern
enterprise hardware engineering. The specification covers the full lifecycle
from system requirements definition and architectural topology to schematic
design, high-speed PCB layout, signal/power integrity validation, and
environmental qualification.

\section{Phase I: System Requirements and Architectural Topology}

Prior to schematic capture, engineering teams establish functional constraints,
thermal envelopes, electrical power budgets, and interface requirements.

\subsection{Core Architectural Subsystems}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Compute Core:} Heterogeneous multi-core SoC or application
    processor paired with Neural Processing Units (NPUs) for edge-AI and
    high-performance workloads.
    \item \textbf{PMIC Tree:} Distributed voltage regulation supplying core logic
    (0.8--1.2\,V), memory (1.1--1.8\,V), and peripheral I/O rails (3.3\,V, 5.0\,V).
    \item \textbf{Mixed-Signal Interconnects:} High-speed differential buses
    including PCIe Gen5/6, USB4, and LPDDR5X, combined with analog sensor
    front-ends.
\end{itemize}

\section{Phase II: Schematic Design and Component Selection}

Schematic entry translates architectural requirements into validated netlists.
Component selection is driven by lifecycle availability, electrical tolerances,
thermal characteristics, and derating constraints.

\subsection{Design Rules and Best Practices}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Derating Analysis:} All components operate at
    $\leq 75\%$ of maximum rated voltage, current, and temperature.
    \item \textbf{Netlist Validation:} Automated ERC checks ensure no floating
    CMOS inputs, ungrounded references, or multi-source driver conflicts.
\end{itemize}

\section{Phase III: PCB Layout and Physical Implementation}

High-speed PCB design requires precise control of trace geometry, impedance,
layer stackup, and electromagnetic behavior.

\subsection{Layer Stackup}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|}
\hline
Layer 1: Top Signal (High-Speed Differential Pairs) \\
Layer 2: Solid Ground Plane (Return Current Reference) \\
Layer 3: Internal Signal / Power Routing \\
Layer 4: Solid Power Plane (Core Rails) \\
Layer 5: Solid Ground Plane (Shielding \& Isolation) \\
Layer 6: Bottom Signal (Low-Speed I/O \& Decoupling) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Signal Integrity (SI) and Power Integrity (PI)}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Impedance Control:} Single-ended traces matched to
    $50\,\Omega \pm 5\%$; differential pairs matched to $100\,\Omega \pm 5\%$.
    \item \textbf{Decoupling Network:} Hierarchical capacitor placement from
    0.1\,$\mu$F to 22\,$\mu$F positioned adjacent to IC power pins to suppress
    high-frequency transients.
\end{itemize}

